# =============================================================================
# _headers — Netlify response headers
# =============================================================================
# Purpose: the installed app must always show the latest published version.
#
# There is no service worker, so nothing caches pages on the device. What CAN
# still serve something stale is the browser's own HTTP cache: without an
# explicit instruction a browser is free to reuse a page it fetched earlier,
# which is how an app icon can open yesterday's version of the site.
#
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate` says: you may keep a copy, but ask before you
# use it. Netlify answers that question with an ETag, so an unchanged page
# comes back as an empty 304 rather than a full download. Opening the app
# stays fast and is guaranteed current.
#
# This is deliberately applied to EVERYTHING rather than just HTML. The CSS
# and JS filenames here don't carry content hashes — main.css is always
# main.css — so caching them aggressively would mean a design change silently
# not arriving. Revalidation costs one small request each.
#
# NOTE: this file starts with an underscore, so Jekyll ignores it by default.
# It is listed under `include:` in _config.yml to get copied into the build.
# =============================================================================

/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The manifest decides the app's name, icon and colours. A phone re-reads it
# when the app is installed and occasionally after; stale is harmless but
# pointless, so it follows the same rule.
/manifest.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  Content-Type: application/manifest+json
